  "usefulness_timing_note": "DOM answer text candidate recorded; usefulness unassessed for error/insufficiency or absent saved kind.",
  "visible_app_text": "Skip to main content\nkeyboard_double_arrow_left\ncoreweaving\n\nYour companion to the Weave docs.\n\nNew conversation\n\nWorkspace\n\nConversation\n\nAnswer library\n\nKeep the conversation going.\n\nAsk a follow-up, such as ‘How would I add that to my agent?’\n\nThe assistant remembers the last six exchanges in this session.\n\nkeyboard_arrow_right\n\nPurpose, permissions & memory\n\nkeyboard_arrow_right\n\nAbout this demo\n\nCOREWEAVING / WEAVE DOCUMENTATION\n\nGood answers leave a trail.\n\nAsk about Weave. See the answer, its sources, and how it was checked.\n\nThis demo records conversations in a public Weave project. Answers are also saved locally for review. Use public examples and keep credentials out of chat.\n\nface\n\nUse the Weave documentation at https://docs.wandb.ai/weave to answer this question:\n\nHow do tracing, evaluations, and Signals fit together?\n\nsmart_toy\n\nPartial answer — points supported by the documentation:\n\nWeave's tracing is built on Ops, Calls, Traces, and Threads [S4]. [S4]\nFor agents, the Agents view gives a turn-by-turn record of conversations, with token usage, tool invocations, and execution spans [S2]. [S2]\nAn Evaluation object defines test examples (a dataset or list) and one or more scoring functions. You run it against a Model or custom function, and each .evaluate() call is an evaluation run [S3]. [S3]\nThe aim is to measure behavior on repeatable test cases, compare changes over time, and spot regressions [S3]. [S3]\n\nStill unresolved:\n\nExplain the role of tracing in Weave and how it relates to evaluations and Signals.\nExplain the role of evaluations in Weave and how they relate to tracing and Signals.\nExplain the role of Signals in Weave and how they relate to tracing and evaluations.\n\nChecked facts · answer incomplete · 3 cited sources · 76.8s \n\nmenu_book\n\nView cited sources\n\nexpand_more\nmanage_search\n\nSources & analysis",
